// XFAIL: DF-236a a static method reached through a FIELD-ACCESS receiver is
// not refused — DF-217q's call-site check has a position gap, and this
// spelling takes the module-qualified `mod.Type.method()` route instead
//
// The mechanism: `_check_method_call`'s `isinstance(expr.object, MemberAccess)`
// branch exists for `module.Struct.method()`, and it decides on the member
// access's TYPE — "this resolves to a struct that has a static of this name" —
// which is equally true of a member access that NAMES the type and one that
// evaluates to a VALUE of it. So `h.inner.nullary()` is routed as though
// `h.inner` were the type: the receiver expression is dropped on the floor and
// the arguments shift by one. Below that means the call silently succeeds and
// prints 7. At arity 1 or more the shift reaches codegen and fails the
// verifier (`internal compiler error ... Type of #1 arg mismatch: i64 !=
// %"Inner"`).
//
// Every OTHER receiver spelling is refused cleanly today, which is what makes
// this a gap and not a missing rule: a plain local, `self`, a call result, a
// tuple element, a `Vector` element, and a field whose type is an ENUM all
// reach the DF-217q error. Only a field access at a STRUCT type takes the
// branch above.
//
// Found by design 236's migration: blade's `self.layout.clean_all()` was
// exactly this shape, one line below a genuine `self.layout.clean()`, and it
// compiled. That call site is fixed; this pins the compiler half.
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: `nullary` is a static method of `Inner` and cannot be called on a value

struct Inner { n: Int }
struct Outer { inner: Inner }

extension Inner {
    static func nullary() -> Int { 7 }
}

func main() {
    let o = Outer(inner: Inner(n: 5))
    print(o.inner.nullary())
}
